<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">stamina</ansi>

Stamina (SP) is your physical endurance, one of your three resource pools,
alongside Health and Conviction. It is the fuel of the body in a fight, and
managing it is the difference between winning a long exchange and gassing
out halfway through one.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ What Stamina Powers ━━━</ansi>

  - <ansi fg="stat">Attacking</ansi>: every swing you throw costs stamina, and a fast
    fighter who strikes several times in a round pays for each blow. A
    flurry costs more than a single strike.
  - <ansi fg="stat">Defending</ansi>: dodging, parrying, and blocking all cost stamina.
  - <ansi fg="stat">Getting back up</ansi>: standing from prone takes a real effort.
  - <ansi fg="stat">Movement</ansi>: travelling costs stamina, and what you are carrying
    raises that price from the very first pound.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The Cost of Defending ━━━</ansi>

A defensive attempt spends stamina, and it spends it whether the defense
succeeds or fails. When a blow comes at you, you meet it with whichever of
your defenses serves you best against that particular strike, and you pay
for that one alone. One incoming swing is never charged to you three times
over.

The three physical defenses are not priced alike. Dodging is the dearest,
because you throw your whole body out of the way. Blocking sits in the
middle, since a shield takes the blow for you. Parrying is the cheapest, as
a weapon already in your hand need only turn the strike aside. A style
built around dodging therefore leans on the most expensive defense in the
game, while a fighter who parries keeps more in the pool for a long fight.

Attacks on your mind and your resolve are turned aside differently.
Steeling yourself against a spell, or standing firm against a taunt, draws
on Conviction rather than Stamina, and the load you carry does not make
either of them harder.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Weight and Skill ━━━</ansi>

Two things change the price of everything physical you do.

The load on your back makes attacking, defending, and travelling dearer. It
begins to cost you from the first pound, but the climb is gentle across
most of the range, so a sensible pack is barely felt. As you approach the
limit of what you can carry, the cost rises steeply, and hauling a full
load into a fight is expensive enough to change how that fight goes. Weight
has no effect on acts of will.

Skill runs the other way. The more practised you are, the less the same
action takes out of you. A beginner pays a little over the going rate, and
a master pays well under half of it. Training a defense makes it cheaper to
use as well as more reliable.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Running Low ━━━</ansi>

Stamina does not fail all at once at some cliff edge. It fades. As the pool
drains, your effectiveness slides down with it, smoothly and steadily: you
swing less often, you land fewer blows, and the blows you do land hit
softer. You keep your defenses even when the pool is empty, and you will
still spend what little is left on them, but a fighter running on nothing
is at a real disadvantage against one who is not. When you feel it going,
break off and recover, or <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi> outright.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Regeneration ━━━</ansi>

Stamina returns a little each round, and much faster while you are out of
combat and at rest, the same rhythm as Health and Conviction. In-combat
recovery is deliberately slow, so extended fights are a test of endurance.
If you are losing the stamina war, disengage and let it come back before
you commit again.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Raising It ━━━</ansi>

Your maximum Stamina is governed by <ansi fg="stat">Vitality</ansi>, which, like every stat on
Gaius, grows through use. The more you fight, endure, and exert, the deeper
the pool becomes and the longer you last before it tells. Some equipment
and buffs raise Vitality (and so stamina) as well.

